WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.270
In 1921, the predominantly
Black neighborhood

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.620
of Greenwood in Tulsa, Oklahoma,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.343
was victim to one of the
biggest massacres on U.S. soil.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.030
More than 1,000

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.280
African-American homes and businesses

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.373
were looted and burned to the ground.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.950
And for decades, the massacre went untold.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.870
White businessmen who ran the city

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.230
realized that Tulsa had a huge PR problem,

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.060
and they were determined
to bury the story.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.958
Stop killing our people!

00:00:39.958 --> 00:00:41.847
Stop killing our people!

00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:44.140
But on the heels of the re-ignition

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.040
of the Black Lives Matter movement,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.540
there's been national attention

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.000
brought to the massacre
through news outlets.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.253
In 1921, a White mob
burned Greenwood to ash.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.140
As well as pop culture.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.063
The story is now getting
the attention it deserves.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.123
But the massacre is
only half of that story.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.180
What happened after the
massacre is just as important.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.210
The real story here is a story

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.940
of the indomitable human spirit.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.870
It's about these remarkable
Black people who had vision.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.980
They created something that
was of national renown.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.690
They watched it destroyed unjustly.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.100
Most of them remained.
They were resilient.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.053
They rebuilt even in the
face of great hostility.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.520
In order to understand
the massacre in Tulsa,

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.263
you have to understand
the context around it.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.470
So the national context

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.410
is something that
sociologists and historians

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.260
often call the low point of
race relations in America

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.180
because there was a proliferation
of so-called race riots.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.740
These were invasions of Black communities

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:09.890
all across the country.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.700
1919 was dubbed Red Summer,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.880
with riots in dozens of
cities around America,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.853
in what was mostly
White on Black violence.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.310
Greenwood, however, dodged those riots

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.510
and actually flourished
because of the oil boom.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.930
More than 10,000 people lived there.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:33.260
People were coming in daily
to this incredible boom town.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.180
This oil city of Tulsa.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.760
Jobs were plentiful.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.760
Plenty of people had
regular steady paychecks.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.330
It was like a main street, really.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.330
So you would have beauty salons
and barbershops, laundries,

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.290
grocery stores, restaurants.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.630
So this was kind of an everyman's paradise

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:59.630
owned by Black folks concentrated
in a 35 square block area

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.323
that abuts downtown Tulsa.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.950
During that era, a
successful Black community

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.920
was met with White resentment

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.480
and Greenwood was no different.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.060
The idea was gain economic
power, gain wealth,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.740
and then you will get respect

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.890
and with respect will
come political power.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.900
Well, just the opposite happened in Tulsa.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.870
If you gain economic power,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.290
you will court resentment
and violence, ultimately.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.510
White Americans can get
very upset and jealous

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.080
when they see African-Americans
who have things, cars,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.850
nice homes, that they don't have.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.860
There's no question that
part of that jealousy

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.100
was part of the fuel

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.800
for what became the Tulsa race massacre.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.053
But it really wasn't
the spark that began it.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.400
That spark was a Black
boy accidentally touching

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.403
a White girl in an elevator.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.010
Tulsa was a tinderbox, a powder keg

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.200
needing only some sort
of igniter or catalyst.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.650
That event involved two
teenagers, Dick Roland,

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.420
19 years old, shoeshine
boy who works downtown.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.410
Sarah Page, 17 years old,

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.340
White girl who operates in the elevator

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.743
in downtown Drexel Building.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.200
What we think happened is

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.110
is that Dick stepped onto the elevator,

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.580
he tripped, he reached his hand out,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.440
he probably grabbed Sarah Page,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.910
she screamed, and he
ran out of the elevator.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.300
Meanwhile, a clerk, a White
clerk in a nearby store,

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.010
had concluded that this had
been an interracial rape attempt

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.053
and he started to spread that story.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:43.620
The next morning,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.630
Dick Rowland was arrested
and taken to the courthouse

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.263
and that could have
been the end of things.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.720
Had it not been for the intervention

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:52.760
of the Tulsa Tribune,

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.227
which the next day published
an article entitled,

00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:58.630
"Nab Negro for Attacking
Girl In an Elevator."

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.960
It was a false narrative
that painted the portrait

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.270
of an attempted rape in broad
daylight in a public space,

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.833
in downtown Tulsa.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.700
After hearing rumors that Dick Roland

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.640
would be lynched,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.830
several dozen Black World
War I vets assembled

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.220
with weapons to confront the mob.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.070
As you might imagine,

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.170
words were exchanged between
the larger White group

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.550
and the smaller Black group.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.340
White man tries to take a Black man's gun,

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.080
the gun discharges.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.540
Chaos ensues.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.853
The violence lasted roughly 16 hours.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.320
Black men put up

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.313
a fearless defense of
the Greenwood community,

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.000
but ultimately, the mob spills over

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.610
into the Greenwood district,
burning, looting, shooting,

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.273
destroying everything in sight.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.940
The destruction of
Greenwood wasn't just bad,

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.340
it was total.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.563
It's just like a wave
that goes block by block.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.870
Both movie theaters go up.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.720
The library branch goes up.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.800
The African-American hospital is burned.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.990
22 churches are burned.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.743
Grocery stores, restaurants,
everything is leveled.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.350
In terms of the number of people who died,

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:16.940
there are reasonable estimates
that go as high as 300.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.303
And Greenwood physically speaking is gone.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.420
Property damage estimates range from 1.5

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:31.053
to $2 million, which translates
to $25 million today.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.200
But this isn't where the story ends.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.340
The fact of the matter is,

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.470
is that Black Tolsons rebuilt Greenwood,

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.270
the community rebuild it.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.990
And that rebuilding was an uphill battle.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.120
With no help from the federal government

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.220
and the city of Tulsa actively trying

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.030
to prevent the rebuilding,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.060
the Greenwood community
began the long, slow

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.360
reconstruction of Greenwood.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.750
The massacre in the Black community

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.640
became a point of pride.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.950
The fact is that they fought back.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.090
So this was a defiant community.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:11.263
It was a proud community and
they were determined to stay.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:14.630
So the community we know

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:18.450
was rebuilt to a significant enough degree

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.840
to host this national conference

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:25.840
of these Black business leaders in 1925.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:29.020
In other words, yes, you might kill us,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.070
yes, you may burn us out,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.190
but we shall not be moved.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.800
We will persevere. We will go forward.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.330
Greenwood did rise again from the ashes

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.560
and by the 1930s and 1940s,
you can make an argument

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.823
that it was even grander
than it was before.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.990
While Greenwood was able to withstand

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.560
and rebuild after their neighborhood

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.990
was raised to the ground,
it wasn't able to withstand

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.110
the second attack on the community,

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:06.170
which was much less obvious,
but no less devious.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:10.030
The first of those attacks
ironically was integration.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.640
And for many of us,
that's counterintuitive

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.920
'cause we embrace integration
as a positive value.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.730
But when integration comes along,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.590
Black dollars flow outside the community.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:22.423
Why?

00:08:22.423 --> 00:08:24.760
Because there are more goods and services

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.670
available to Black folks,

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.390
at better price points for Black folks,

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:32.943
and it undermines the financial
foundation of the community.

00:08:39.682 --> 00:08:40.560
The other factor

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.710
that destroyed Greenwood
for a second time?

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.220
Urban renewal.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:50.040
Urban renewal projects
often have negative effects

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:54.120
on communities of color because
those communities are often

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:59.120
unable to up against urban
planning done by people

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:03.950
who don't take into consideration
the thoughts and values

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.513
of communities of color.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:09.800
One of those projects?

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.190
An eight-lane Interstate freeway

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.790
was plowed right through deep Greenwood,

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.323
through the heart of there.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:21.540
It divided the community in such a way

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.370
that it's virtually
impossible for the community

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:27.490
to come back together as a cohesive whole

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.163
like it was before the
location of the highway.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.550
And Greenwood wasn't the only city

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.403
destroyed by these freeways.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:46.140
Chicago, Detroit, Durham,
the Bronx, Los Angeles,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.360
all suffered similar devastating
effects from freeways

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.060
essentially ripping a hole

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:55.110
directly through Black communities
during the '50s and '60s.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.950
It's the old cliche,

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.270
if you're not at the
table, you're on the menu.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:00.950
Certainly, I hope that's changing

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:02.780
with movements like the
Black Lives Matter movement

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.270
and what we're seeing today.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.790
But in the past, a lot of
these communities of color

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:08.690
just didn't have a place at the table,

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:10.170
didn't have a voice,

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.653
and didn't have the number of White allies

00:10:12.653 --> 00:10:14.673
that I hope that we have today.

